              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 12.8: Food chain"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.594,
            0.883,
            0.65
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "How do plants get their food? As you know, plants make their own food by the process of photosynthesis. Thus, they are called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "producers",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " or "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "autotrophs",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (auto=self + troph=food)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.652,
            0.883,
            0.724
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Organisms that cannot produce their own food and depend on other organisms for their food are called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "consumers",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " or "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "heterotrophs",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (hetero = other + troph = food). List the heterotrophs from Table 12.4."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.253,
            0.725,
            0.883,
            0.8
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Organisms that eat only plants are called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "herbivores",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", such as deer and hare. Those that eat only animals are "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "carnivores",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", such as leopard. Organisms that eat both plants and animals are "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "omnivores",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", such as crows, foxes, and mice."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.274,
            0.811,
            0.585,
            0.836
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "12.5 Who Eats Whom?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
